  "verbalization": "Lomitapide is shown to have a target relationship with Alpha-2C adrenergic receptor through multiple pathways, all of which converge on a common intermediate molecule: sodium 2-[[2-[[hydroxy-(3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-methyloxan-2-yl)oxyphosphoryl]amino]-4-methylpentanoyl]amino]-3-(1H-indol-3-yl)propanoic acid. This compound is identified as a target of lomitapide and is itself a target of the Alpha-2C adrenergic receptor. The evidence suggests that lomitapide may act through a mechanism involving this intermediate molecule, which is then targeted by the Alpha-2C adrenergic receptor. The presence of multiple independent pathways linking lomitapide to the same target, with consistent scores across different paths, supports a plausible mechanism of action. The core mechanism appears to involve the modulation of adrenergic signaling through the Alpha-2C receptor, potentially through the inhibition of a downstream effector or the direct interaction with the receptor. The evidence does not provide direct evidence of lomitapide's mechanism of action at the receptor level, but the shared pathway and the presence of a common target suggest a mechanistic link.",
